# Local-only working artifacts — deliberately NOT in the repo (2026-08-02).
# All of these still live on disk; they are simply things the running site never
# loads, and they accounted for 19.4 MB of a 29 MB repo.
#
#  · raw/superseded source downloads for the hero background
#  · our own render screenshots (regenerate them; don't commit them)
#  · the target's real video + poster, kept locally as a grading reference only
#
# `assets/measurements.json` IS tracked — it is small and is the CDP evidence
# that features/hero/CONTEXT.md cites.
features/*/assets/*.mp4
features/*/assets/render-*.png
public/video/hero-original.mp4
public/video/hero-poster-original.jpg

# Client testimonial MASTERS as supplied (2026-08-05) — 312 MB of 1080x1920 h264 and one
# 4K HEVC .MOV. The web encodes in public/testimonials/ are 21 MB and ARE tracked, because
# the running site loads them. These masters are the re-encode source and nothing more;
# keep them on disk, out of the repo. Crop boxes needed to reproduce the encode are in
# features/testimonials/CONTEXT.md, so this folder is replaceable, not load-bearing.
Client testimonials/

# Discovery font SOURCES (2026-08-07). ⚠️ THIS REPO IS PUBLIC — verified with
# `gh repo view --json visibility` before writing this rule.
#
# Discovery is a COMMERCIAL Fontshok release and the user supplied DESKTOP .ttf files
# (8 statics + a variable font, ~1.5 MB). Committing those to a public repo redistributes a
# paid font to anyone who clones it, which is a different and much larger problem than a
# website serving its own webfont.
#
# So: the sources stay on disk in assets/fonts/discovery/, and the ONLY font byte in the
# repo is public/fonts/discovery/discovery-var.woff2 — the one file the running site has to
# fetch to render at all. Same reasoning as the testimonial masters above: keep them local,
# out of the repo, and document how to regenerate. The fontTools one-liner that rebuilds the
# woff2 from the .ttf is in src/app/fonts-discovery.css.
#
# ⚠️ SEPARATE, UNRESOLVED: whether a desktop licence permits web embedding AT ALL. It
# usually does not — that is normally a separate webfont licence. This rule limits the
# exposure; it does not settle the question. See docs/DESIGN-SYSTEM.md.
assets/fonts/
